---
mm: 1.0
kind: doc
name: modules.cpp abstract data model
---

# Purpose
models/ is an abstract C++20 module hierarchy that models this project's own
manifest-driven structure, tools, workflow, and generated output as data. It
is a second, independent description of the same facts docs/modules.mdy
gives in prose: an abstract interface a program can query, rather than a
document a person reads. mm.model (modules/mm/model) adapts the real project
onto that interface, and tools/model uses it to check the project against
itself.

Like everything else in the project, models/ follows docs/modules-c++20.mdy:
modules instead of headers, no exceptions, no new templates, enum class,
[[nodiscard]] on accessors, and explicit virtual destructors on every base.

# The eight modules
Eleven modules, each under models/<name>/<name>.cppm with its own mm.mdy, in
dependency order:

- models.document — front matter and body blocks for one MDY file (models::Document, models::Block). Foundational: every MDY file is a document, including a manifest, so everything else sits above this.
- models.configuration — the project's build configuration (models::Configuration). Every accessor is declared policy, not a measurement of an actual run: compiler()/compiler_flags()/linker_flags()/ verbose() mirror the live mm::build::Toolchain, honoring $CXX, but that is only the self hosted build's rule — bootstrap.sh and build0 deliberately use a plain "c++" and never read $CXX, so that bootstrap reaches a working build1 the same way on every machine. Those are two intentional rules, not an inconsistency, and this type describes the self hosted one. platform()/locale()/shell() state fixed project policy that is documented (POSIX, C, /bin/sh) but, for locale() specifically, not enforced anywhere (no setlocale(3), no LC_ALL/LANG set); platform() and shell() are closer to actually true everywhere (mm::build::run always execs /bin/sh) but are still policy this type declares rather than measures. The model represents the whole project concept, including facts that are implied and fixed rather than actively checked, but "policy" and "observed invocation configuration" are deliberately not the same claim: see models/configuration/configuration.cppm's own comment for the accessor-by-accessor detail. Foundational and independent: a Configuration is an input to running a tool, not a structural fact about the repository.
- models.manifest — the manifest kind taxonomy docs/modules.mdy defines: project, dir, module, app, test, doc (models::Kind, models::ManifestNode, models::BuildableNode, models::TranslationUnit, and one class per kind). ManifestNode, BuildableNode and TranslationUnit deliberately carry the same names as the mm::build structs they abstract, so one concept reads under one name in both layers; the one place the sets differ is kind:doc, which mm::build::BuildableNode also holds but models::DocNode does not, since a doc's file: entries are prose rather than sources. Imports models.document, because every manifest is one.
- models.repository — the aggregate view over a whole manifest tree (models::Repository): a project's root plus every module, app, test, and doc reachable from it. Imports models.manifest.
- models.tool — an invocable tool (models::Tool), distinct from models::ModuleNode's declaration: a Tool may have no declaring manifest at all, such as a third party tool or the pre-manifest bootstrap artifacts build0/build1. Imports models.manifest.
- models.modules — a resolved C++20 module (models::Module), distinct from models::ModuleNode the same way: ModuleNode's uses() is dependency names as text, Module's imports() is those names resolved to other Module objects. Imports models.manifest.
- models.workflow — a workflow step (models::Operation): one of the top level *.sh scripts. Not a single depends_on() DAG: invokes(branch) is an ordered, possibly repeating sequence of Tools per branch (most operations have one branch; bootstrap.sh has two, since it tries build0 and only falls back to raw compiler calls if that leaves no build1), and requires_artifacts()/produces() are the input and output side of what a single "must run first" edge used to conflate — build.sh's real requirement is that out/bin/build exists, not that bootstrap.sh ran in the current session (bootstrap.sh's own final step already builds and installs it too, which is why build.sh is not the only thing that can satisfy this). role() is a three way Role (Required, Optional, UserInitiated) rather than a bool: clean.sh is not "optional" the way check.sh is, it sits outside the forward sequence entirely. ArtifactKind also lives here, not in models.artifacts: models.artifacts depends on models.workflow for produced_by(), so the reverse dependency is unavailable. Imports models.tool.
- models.artifacts — a generated artifact (models::GeneratedArtifact), starting from docs/modules.mdy's "Generated artifacts" section but splitting AppObject and ToolObject each into an object-file and an executable variant, plus Staged for out/build0 and out/build1, and the Operation whose script produces it. Imports models.workflow.

# Design choices worth knowing
- Every module is a single export module, not one class per module, following mm.build's own precedent of bundling a tightly coupled family of types together rather than splitting each class out.
- A declaration is not the thing it declares. models::AppNode and models::Tool both exist because a manifest saying an app should exist is not the same fact as a binary existing to run; models::ModuleNode and models::Module split the same way, for uses: as text versus imports() as a resolved graph. This is the recurring shape across models.tool and models.modules.
- Nothing here executes anything. There is deliberately no run() on Tool or Operation: invoking things is mm.build's and mm.test's job. models/ describes what exists and how it relates, not how to act on it.

# mm.model: the adapter
modules/mm/model adapts mm::build's and mm::mdy's real data onto
models::Repository and models::Tool, rather than duplicating either. Its one
exported type, mm::model::Loaded, owns every concrete node, document, and
tool it builds, so pointers returned through the models:: interfaces stay
valid for Loaded's lifetime.

Loaded::tools() gives one models::Tool per kind:app manifest, each
Provenance::BuiltIn with declared_by() pointing at that app's AppNode and
invocation() set to out/bin/<name>, matching what build.sh actually
installs, plus two fixed entries: build0 (declared_by() == nullptr,
tools/build/main.cpp has no manifest at all) and build1 (declared_by()
pointing at the same AppNode as out/bin/build, since bootstrap.sh compiles
build1 from tools/build/build.cpp, the exact file tools/build/mm.mdy
declares — build1 and out/bin/build are two Tools for one declared app,
built at two different bootstrap stages). Third party tools (cppcheck,
semgrep) are still not represented: they are not fixed, project-known paths
the way build0/build1 are, so populating them would mean inventing data
rather than adapting or stating a known fact.

`Loaded::load(root, ok)` walks the project the same way tools/check does: one
manifest tree from root, which reaches tests/ through the root manifest's
folder: tests entry like every other subtree. Each node is given a real
document() by re-parsing its own mm.mdy through mm::mdy::Parser, since
mm::build::BuildableNode does not keep the MDYDocument it read.

parent() and children() are resolved for real, by also walking
mm::build::load_nodes and cross referencing its structural ManifestNode list against
the BuildableNode lists by directory: a node built from load_nodes carries its
parent's and children's indices into a shared table, filled in as every node
is constructed, so each node's parent()/children() looks up a real sibling
rather than returning nullptr or empty. root() is the manifest load_nodes
visited first; if that manifest is not kind:project, load() fails rather
than invent a project identity, since Repository::root() is typed
ProjectNode&. tests/mm/model exercises this against the project's own real
tree.

Loaded::resolved_modules() gives one models::Module per ModuleNode, built in
two passes: first one Module per declared module, then each one's
imports() resolved by matching declared_by().uses() text against every
other Module's exported_module_name(). Two passes because an import can
name a module constructed after it — there is no declaration order that
guarantees otherwise. load() itself never calls mm::build::order() (the
only other place a use: naming an unknown module is normally caught), so a
use: entry that does not resolve here fails the whole load() outright
(ok = false, with a "uses unknown module" diagnostic on stderr) rather than
coming back as a Module whose imports() is silently shorter than
declared_by().uses(). tests/mm/model pins this against a scratch tree built
specifically to have one such edge.

# tools/model: the consistency tool
tools/model is a kind:app target, use: mm.model, installed as out/bin/model.
It checks the real project against itself through models::Repository and
models::Tool alone, never touching mm::build's structures directly, so what
it reports is whatever the abstract model itself would tell a reader:

- dependency resolution — every use: entry on a module, app, or test names a module: some kind:module manifest actually exports. mm::build::order already rejects an unknown module at build time; this re-derives the same fact independently, through the model, as a check that does not require a full build to run.
- build completeness — every BuiltIn models::Tool's invocation() exists on disk: what is declared matches what has actually been built. Expressed over Tool rather than AppNode, so it covers out/bin/<name> for every kind:app manifest and, now that mm.model represents them, out/build0 and out/build1 too. ThirdParty tools such as c++ are skipped: invocation() is a bare command resolved from $PATH for them, not a root relative path, so checking it against root would always fail.

Module cache is reported alongside the two checks, but informationally, not
as a violation: gcm.cache has no declaring Tool or manifest — every compiler
invocation writes to it as a side effect — and its absence is legitimate any
time nothing has compiled a module yet, such as right after clean.sh. This
is deliberately not the fuller GeneratedArtifact/models.artifacts machinery
once considered for this: InstalledBinary and Staged are already fully
covered by the Tool-based check above, so the only remaining fact worth
stating for ModuleCache is a single existence check.

--configuration additionally reports models::Configuration, via
mm::model::default_configuration(verbose): the live compiler/flags (honoring
$CXX) plus the fixed platform/locale/shell policy. Independent of the
manifest tree — default_configuration() takes no root_dir — so it is
reported even when the checks above fail to load one.

--tools additionally lists every Loaded::tools() entry: name, provenance(),
invocation(), and declared_by()'s name or "(none)". Needs the tree loaded,
unlike --configuration, so it is reported alongside the two checks rather
than independent of them.

Common commands are:

- ./out/bin/model
- ./out/bin/model -v
- ./out/bin/model modules/mm.mdy
- ./out/bin/model --configuration
- ./out/bin/model --tools

Both checks are structural, derived from models::Repository, rather than
textual parsing of docs/modules.mdy's prose. A textual check against that
prose (for example, "is every *.sh script mentioned in the Repository layout
tree") is future work, not something tools/model does today.

# Boundaries
- models.artifacts still has no adapter: mm.model builds models.document, models.manifest, models.repository, models.configuration, models.tool, and, as of Loaded::operations(), models.workflow. A tool that needs GeneratedArtifact data (an actual filesystem scan of out/, cross checked against requires_artifacts()/produces()) must extend mm.model first.
- mm.model's models.tool coverage is kind:app manifests plus three fixed, project-known entries: build0, build1, and c++ (Provenance::ThirdParty). cppcheck and semgrep are still absent: unlike c++, nothing in this project's own scripts names them at a fixed path the way bootstrap.sh names c++, so representing them would mean inventing data rather than stating a known fact.
- Loaded::operations() is fixed, hand authored data mirroring the seven *.sh scripts, the same reasoning as build0/build1/c++ in tools(): nothing here is derived from a manifest walk. invokes() only names Tools; the fallback branch inside bootstrap's second invokes() sequence is not decomposed further than "c++, invoked six times" — it does not know or care that those six invocations are five compiles and one link, the same abstraction boundary that already applies to how build.sh's own invokes() does not enumerate what build1 compiles internally.
- tools/model does not check docs/modules.mdy's prose against reality (for example, its Repository layout tree, or the Build and development workflow script list); it checks only what models::Repository can already answer.
